Tricks / Details 
================
[a] For the most part, Store Forwarding is entirely predictable and per Amir R's paper
    once discovered can be stored in i-cache bit. Going fwd, this avoids storing
    all stores in the Store Buffer or having all Loads lookup Store Buffer.
    Of course you ll still need to validate LMQ with SQ before retirement and squash
    if necessary (and relearn)

[b] AMD Barcelona appears to do LSU1 lookup for loads in parallel to L1 dTLB lookup
    (neat right? :-))

[c] Squashing can be full or partial, the latter being only on those instructions
    that used the incorrect load value. This is actually quite difficult because
    it requires maintaining instruction dependency (register) information past
    execution. Full squashing involves flushing and restarting all instructions
    past the failed load in program order.

[d] Prediction techniques involve predicting a certain load that is likely to 
    collide therefore it should wait until all prior ambiguous stores'have had
    their addresses computed.

    Or you can predict exact load-stores that collide and use a register to
    capture fwding. None of the above would require associative searches of LSQ
    but do require squashing.
    